Folio (test build 0.0.1-test42)
===============================

A reader for PDF and ebook files (EPUB, FB2, MOBI, CBZ, XPS), built on
MuPDF. This is an early test: it opens and shows documents, selects and
copies text, and looks words up. Markup, notebooks and the rest come later.

The bookshelf
  Click Select on Folio's icon bar icon (or choose Bookshelf... from its
  menu) to see your bookshelf: the covers of your books, with how far
  you've read each. Double-click a cover to open it.
  To add books, drag them to the bookshelf, or choose On bookshelf from a
  document's menu. Drag a directory to the bookshelf to have Folio watch
  it: Folio looks in it, and the directories inside, for new documents
  each time it starts and when you choose Refresh from the bookshelf's
  menu. Watched folders... on that menu lists them (click Stop watching to
  drop one).
  Remove from bookshelf (or Delete) only takes a book off the shelf; the
  file isn't touched. Books whose files can't be found are shown grey;
  Remove missing books takes them all off. Sort by recent, title or
  author from the menu. The shelf is kept in <Choices$Write>.Folio.Shelf
  and the covers in <Choices$Write>.Folio.Covers.

Opening a document
  Drag a file to Folio's icon on the icon bar, or double-click a PDF while
  Folio is running. A document that is already open comes to the front.
  If a PDF needs a password, Folio asks for it.

In a document window
  Scroll as usual. Page Down / Page Up    next / previous page
  Home / End     first / last page        Up / Down   scroll a little
  Ctrl-N         dark page on and off     Ctrl-F2     close the window
  F4             Find                     Ctrl-Left   back from a link
  Print          Print...                 F5          Go to page
  Menu           View: Dark page, Sepia page, Zoom in, Zoom out,
                 Actual size, Fit width, Two pages. Navigate: the page
                 moves above, Go to page, Contents and Back. Find... and
                 Define "word".
  Close with Adjust also opens the document's directory.
  F11 (or View > Full screen) fills the screen with the document, with no
  title bar, scroll bars or toolbar; F11 or Escape returns to the desktop.
  Pages of different sizes are each shown at their own size (a book
  whose cover is smaller than its pages, a landscape page among upright
  ones). Folio measures the pages in the background after opening.
  Default scale: zoom a window to the scale you like, then choose View >
  Use this scale for new documents. Every document opens at that scale
  from then on (also in the Choices window).

The toolbar and thumbnails
  The toolbar along the top of each document window has, from the left:
  thumbnails on/off, Contents, previous page, the page number (type a
  number and press Return to go there), next page, zoom out, the scale,
  zoom in, fit width, two pages, dark page, Find, Define, Highlight, Underline,
  Strike out and Note (and Read aloud, with the Speech plugin). Rest the
  pointer on a button for a moment to see its name. View > Toolbar hides
  or shows it.
  Windows open wide enough for every button. If you make one narrower,
  arrows appear at the toolbar's right end: click them (Adjust goes the
  other way), or use the mouse wheel over the toolbar, to scroll it.
  The thumbnails drawer (View > Thumbnails, or the first toolbar button)
  shows small pages down the left of the window, which widens to make room
  for it (and narrows again when it closes); the page you're reading
  is framed in blue, and a click on a thumbnail goes to that page.

Two pages side by side
  View > Two pages (or the two-pages toolbar button) shows the pages in
  pairs; the window widens to make room, and narrows again when you go
  back to one. Page Down and Page Up then move a pair at a time. With
  First page alone ticked (as it is to start with), the first page sits
  on its own on the right and the rest pair up as in a printed book; untick
  it for pages 1 and 2 side by side.

Notes and markings (PDFs)
  Select some text, then click Highlight, Underline or Strike out on the
  toolbar (or use the Edit menu). A selection over several pages is marked
  on each of them (Ctrl-Z then undoes one page at a time). Note adds a note at the selection, or at
  the top of the page; click on a note to read, change or delete it.
  Click on a highlight to select it; Delete removes it.
  Ctrl-Z undoes the last change and Ctrl-Y redoes it.
  F3 (or Save... on the menu) saves the PDF with its notes and markings;
  the window's title shows * while there are changes to save, and
  closing the window or quitting asks first. Ebooks can't be marked yet.

Reading aloud (with the Speech plugin)
  Drag the !Folio in FolioSpeech-<version>.zip over this !Folio (the Filer
  adds Plugins.Speech to it), then run Folio again. A speaker button
  then appears at the right of the toolbar, and Read aloud, Speak selection
  and Reading speed on the document's menu. Without the plugin there's no
  speech, and nothing for it is loaded. Speech.ReadMe says what it needs
  (SharedSoundBuffer and StreamManager among them).
  Read aloud starts at the top of what you can see of the page, or at the
  sentence the selection is in, and goes on through the document; the
  sentence being read is shown with a tinted band, and the pages turn as it
  goes. Click the button (or Read aloud) again to stop. Speak selection
  reads just the selected text.

Scanned pages (with the OCR plugin)
  A scanned PDF is pictures of pages, with no text to find or select.
  Drag the !Folio in FolioOCR-<version>.zip over this !Folio (the Filer
  adds Plugins.OCR to it), then run Folio again: the document's menu gets
  Text recognition. Folio then reads the text in each scanned page as you
  come to it (the window's title says when), and Find, selecting, Copy,
  Define and Read aloud work on it. Recognise all pages does the whole
  document in the background, so Find can search all of it; Recognise this
  page does just the page you're on. Accurate makes fewer mistakes; Fast
  takes about half as long. What's recognised is kept, so it isn't done
  again next time. OCR.ReadMe has more.

Filling in forms (PDFs)
  Click on a field to fill it in. A text field opens for typing over the
  page: Return or Tab keeps what you type (Tab then goes to the next
  field, Shift-Tab back), Escape leaves it as it was. In a field of several
  lines, Return and the arrow keys move between them. A click on a check
  box or radio button ticks it; a list or drop-down field shows its
  choices as a menu. Ctrl-Z undoes each change; F3 saves the PDF with
  what you've filled in. Fields worked out by the form (totals, dates
  checked as you type) need its JavaScript, which is off unless Choices
  turn it on; Folio never sends a form anywhere.

Printing
  Print... on the document's menu (or the Print key) opens the Print box:
  all pages or some, copies, scale, upright or sideways, and draft. It
  needs !Printers loaded with a printer chosen. Each page is made as big as
  it really is at 100% (smaller if it wouldn't fit the paper), in the
  middle of the paper; pages are always printed white, with your notes and
  markings. Printing goes on in the background while you carry on: the
  window's title shows which page it's on. The machine only pauses while
  the printer driver takes each finished page. Choose Print... again (or
  close the window) to stop it.

Document info
  Document info... on the document's menu shows the file's name, where it
  is and its size, then what the document says about itself: title,
  author, subject, keywords, the program that made it, when it was made
  and changed, its PDF version, pages and page size, its security and what
  its author allows (printing, copying, changing, notes...), and whether it
  has a form, attached files or JavaScript.

Restrictions (PDFs)
  A PDF's author can say it mustn't be printed, copied from or filled in.
  Folio keeps to that: Print, Copy, Export page as Draw, filling in its
  form and (if the author doesn't allow text for screen readers either)
  Read aloud say so instead, and offer to lift it with the document's
  owner password. To have Folio ignore such restrictions, untick "Keep to
  a PDF's limits on printing and copying" in the Choices window.

Security
  Folio asks before following a link out of a document (to a web page, an
  email or another file), showing where it goes; links to other programs
  are never followed. When it opens a PDF with JavaScript, attached files,
  or actions that would start a program or send the form somewhere, it
  says so; it doesn't do any of those things. A form's JavaScript runs
  only if the Choices window allows it, and can't reach your files or the
  network.

Choices
  Choices... on Folio's icon bar menu opens the Choices window: how new
  documents open (scale, page colour, two pages, the toolbar), whether
  links are underlined, the
  reading-aloud speed, text recognition (Accurate or Fast, and whether
  scanned pages are recognised as they're shown), and the security
  choices above. Save uses and keeps them (in
  <Choices$Write>.Folio.Choices); Default fills in Folio's own settings.

Fonts
  PDFs usually carry their fonts. For one that doesn't, and for scripts
  Folio has no font of its own for (Chinese, Japanese, Korean), it uses
  the computer's fonts through fontconfig: PackMan's FontConfig setup if
  it's installed, else the fonts in !Folio.Fonts (Fonts.ReadMe says more).

Passwords (PDFs)
  Password... on a PDF's menu shows whether it has a password, and puts
  one on or takes it off. Type the new password twice and click Set
  password; the Save box then opens, and the password is on the file once
  it's saved (Folio writes the whole file again, encrypted with AES-256).
  Remove password saves it without one. A PDF that anyone can open but
  that has an owner password needs that password first.
  Don't forget the password: without it, nobody can open the file.
  Setting a password needs the CryptRandom module, which Folio loads from
  !System; saving with a new password, or without one, also undoes any
  digital signatures in the file.

Export page as Draw
  Export page as Draw... on the document's menu saves the page you're
  reading as a Draw file: drag its icon to a directory, or straight into
  Draw. Lines and shapes become Draw paths; text becomes outlines (it looks
  the same without the document's fonts); each picture becomes a sprite of
  its own, at its own resolution, cut to the part that shows, so you can
  take it out and save it on its own. Gradients become sprites too.
  Draw can't clip or blend, so a few things differ from the page: see-
  through effects are lost, and highlighter marks go under the text.

Opening at a page
  To open a document at a given page, from an Obey file, a program or the
  command line, set Folio$OpenPage first:
      Set Folio$OpenPage 12
      Filer_Run SDFS::Disc.$.Docs.Manual/pdf
  This works whether or not Folio is running; the variable is unset once
  used. Starting Folio with Run <Folio$Dir>.!Run -page 12 <file> does the
  same. (A book's later pages are counted first, so it may take a moment.)

Links, Contents and Find
  Links have a blue line under them (untick "Underline links" in the
  Choices window to have them as the document draws them).
  Click a link to follow it: a link to another part of the document goes
  there; a web or email link opens in your browser or mail program.
  Dragging from a link selects text as usual. Navigate > Back (Ctrl-Left)
  returns to where you were, link after link.
  Navigate > Contents... lists the document's chapters and sections, with
  their page numbers; the part you're reading is highlighted. Click on one
  to go there.
  F5 (or Navigate > Go to page...) asks for a page number and goes there;
  Back returns from it as from a link.
  F4 (or Find... on the menu) opens the Find box. Type the text and press
  Return (Next) to find it; Previous goes back. Capitals don't matter.
  The search starts at the page you're reading and goes round the end of
  the document once. What it finds is selected, so you can copy it or
  look it up.

Selecting text and looking words up
  Drag with Select to select text; double-click a word to select it;
  Adjust extends the selection. A selection can run on over the next
  pages (or back over earlier ones): drag past the window's top or bottom
  and it scrolls, faster the further out you go. Ctrl-C copies it to the clipboard,
  Ctrl-A selects the whole page, Escape clears the selection.
  With one word selected, Menu shows Define "word": its meanings from
  Wiktionary (CC BY-SA) appear in a small box near the word. Copy copies
  them; clicking "Wiktionary" opens the whole entry in your browser.
  Looking words up needs the network; Folio loads the URL_Fetcher,
  AcornHTTP and AcornSSL modules from !System the first time. Words
  already looked up are kept in
  <Choices$Write>.Folio.Dict and work offline.

Folio reopens each document at the page where you left it, at the same
scale, pages across, and window size and place (kept in
<Choices$Write>.Folio.Places). Pages are drawn by a background thread, so
the desktop keeps going while a heavy page loads.

Ebooks are laid out to a 400 x 600 point page at 11 point text. Folio
counts a book's pages in the background the first time, then remembers
them in <Choices$Write>.Folio.Layouts, so it opens at once next time.

Needs a 32 thousand or more colour screen mode in this test build.

If anything goes wrong, the file <Wimp$ScrapDir>.FolioLog says what
Folio did; please send it with your report.

